; smc.a -- a program that GENUINELY MODIFIES ITS OWN CODE.
;
; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
; --------------------
; Phase 30's criterion 3 is explicit that EMITTING the `=*+$01` idiom is not
; the criterion. A fixture that merely CONTAINS the idiom proves only that the
; exporter can print three characters. What has to be proved is that a program
; whose own bytes are rewritten at runtime exports with its write target named
; by a mid-instruction label, and REASSEMBLES BYTE-IDENTICALLY.
;
; So this program really does it. On every pass:
;
;   * `inc smc_operand` increments the byte at $0802, which is the IMMEDIATE
;     OPERAND of the `lda #$00` at $0801. The instruction the CPU executes on
;     the next pass is a different instruction than the one it executed on this
;     one -- `lda #$00`, then `lda #$01`, then `lda #$02`, ...
;   * that value is written to the border colour register, so the effect is
;     observable and the self-modification is not dead code.
;   * `jmp $0801` runs the modified instruction.
;
; The label `smc_operand` therefore names an address that is STRICTLY INSIDE a
; decoded instruction. There is no way to name it with an ordinary
; `name = $0802` definition and still have an exporter round-trip: the
; assembler must be told the address relative to the program counter at the
; instruction that OWNS the byte.
;
; PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING
; -------------------------
; `smc_operand = *+$01` sits on its own line IMMEDIATELY BEFORE its host
; instruction. Moved to immediately AFTER it, the label names $0804 -- the
; operand of the NEXT instruction -- and ACME exits 0 either way. Only a
; byte-diff tells the two apart. `anno-export-asm.test.ts` carries that
; negative control.
;
; ASSEMBLED WITH `-f cbm`, so `smc.prg` carries the two-byte little-endian load
; address ($01 $08) a real C64 `.prg` has.
;
; Regenerate with:
;   cd src/mcp/vice && node fixtures/export-asm/make-export-asm-fixtures.mjs

!cpu 6510

* = $0801

smc_operand = *+$01
        lda #$00
        inc smc_operand
        sta $d020
        jmp $0801
